<h1>DaVinci Resolve 21 System Requirements 2026: Minimum vs Recommended (Complete Hardware Guide)</h1>
<p><strong>Last verified:</strong> July 2026 against Blackmagic Design's official "About DaVinci Resolve Studio" documentation and techspecs page.</p>

<p>DaVinci Resolve 21 is GPU-hungry — more than any editor you've probably used before. This guide breaks down Blackmagic Design's <em>official minimum specs</em>, what actually works in practice, and three build tiers (budget / mid-range / pro) so you don't waste money on the wrong component.</p>

<div class="callout">
<strong>Quick Answer:</strong> Officially, Resolve 21 needs 16GB RAM (Windows) / 8GB (Apple Silicon Mac), a GPU with 4GB VRAM, and Windows 10+ / macOS 15 (Apple Silicon only — Intel Macs are no longer supported on Resolve 21) / Rocky Linux 8.6. In real-world 4K color grading, you want 32GB RAM and 8GB+ VRAM minimum.
</div>

<h2>1. Official Minimum System Requirements (Blackmagic Design)</h2>

<table>
<tr><th>Component</th><th>Windows</th><th>macOS</th><th>Linux</th></tr>
<tr><td>OS Version</td><td>Windows 10 (Creators Update) or later</td><td>macOS 15 Sequoia or later — <strong>Apple Silicon only</strong> (Resolve 21 dropped Intel Mac support)</td><td>Rocky Linux 8.6 or CentOS 7.3+</td></tr>
<tr><td>RAM</td><td>16 GB (32 GB when using Fusion)</td><td>8 GB (16 GB when using Fusion)</td><td>16 GB minimum</td></tr>
<tr><td>GPU / VRAM</td><td>Integrated or discrete GPU, 4 GB VRAM minimum, OpenCL 1.2 or CUDA 12.8 support</td><td>Any Apple Silicon GPU (Metal support built-in)</td><td>Discrete GPU, 4 GB VRAM minimum</td></tr>
<tr><td>GPU Driver</td><td>NVIDIA Studio Driver 570.65 or newer (not Game Ready driver)</td><td>N/A — handled by macOS</td><td>Latest AMD/NVIDIA official Linux driver</td></tr>
<tr><td>Display</td><td>1280 x 1024 minimum</td><td>1280 x 1024 minimum</td><td>1280 x 1024 minimum</td></tr>
</table>

<p class="note">These are the numbers that let Resolve <em>open and run</em> — not what you need for a real 4K color grading session. See section 3 for that.</p>

<h2>2. Minimum vs Recommended (Real-World Practical Table)</h2>

<table>
<tr><th>Use Case</th><th>CPU</th><th>RAM</th><th>GPU / VRAM</th><th>Storage</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Minimum</strong> (HD cuts, basic edits)</td><td>Quad-core Intel i5 / Ryzen 5</td><td>16 GB</td><td>4 GB VRAM (integrated OK)</td><td>256 GB SSD</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Recommended</strong> (HD–light 4K, ProRes/DNxHR)</td><td>6-core Intel 12th Gen+ / Ryzen 5000+</td><td>16–32 GB</td><td>8 GB VRAM (RTX 3060/4060)</td><td>512 GB–1 TB NVMe SSD</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Professional</strong> (4K–8K grading, heavy Fusion, AI tools)</td><td>8–16 core Intel Core Ultra 9 / Ryzen 9 9000-series</td><td>32–64 GB</td><td>16–24 GB VRAM (RTX 4080 Super / 4090)</td><td>1–2 TB NVMe SSD + RAID/NAS for media</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="callout">
<strong>Common mistake:</strong> Buying a single 32GB RAM stick instead of two 16GB sticks in dual-channel. This halves your memory bandwidth — Resolve depends heavily on bandwidth to feed frames to the GPU. Always buy RAM in matched pairs (dual-channel).
</div>

<h2>3. Why GPU Matters More Than CPU in Resolve</h2>
<p>Resolve offloads almost all color grading, Fusion compositing, Neural Engine AI (Magic Mask, Super Scale, noise reduction), and playback rendering to the GPU. A weak CPU with a strong GPU will usually outperform a strong CPU with a weak GPU. If you're budgeting, prioritize spending in this order:</p>
<ol>
<li>GPU (VRAM first, then core count)</li>
<li>RAM (dual-channel, 32GB minimum for serious work)</li>
<li>Storage (NVMe SSD for OS + active project; separate drive for media)</li>
<li>CPU (only matters heavily for H.265/HEVC decode without GPU hardware decoding)</li>
</ol>

