<h2>5. FAQ</h2>

<div class="faq">
<h4>Can I run DaVinci Resolve with 8GB RAM?</h4>
<p>Only on Apple Silicon Macs — that's the official minimum there. On Windows, 16GB is the official floor, and even that is tight since your OS and background apps eat into it before Resolve opens.</p>

<h4>Do I need DaVinci Resolve Studio for better performance?</h4>
<p>No — the free and Studio versions share the same rendering engine. Studio adds GPU-accelerated Neural Engine AI tools and advanced noise reduction, which are more resource-hungry, but the base performance ceiling is identical.</p>

<h4>Is a laptop good enough for professional color grading?</h4>
<p>Yes, if it has a dedicated GPU (RTX 3060+ or Apple Silicon M-series) and at least 16GB RAM (32GB preferred for Fusion-heavy work).</p>

<h4>Does DaVinci Resolve 21 still support Intel Macs?</h4>
<p>No. Resolve 21 requires an Apple Silicon Mac running macOS 15 Sequoia or later. Intel Mac users should stay on DaVinci Resolve 20.</p>

<h4>NVIDIA Game Ready vs Studio driver — does it matter?</h4>
<p>Yes. Always install the NVIDIA Studio Driver, not Game Ready. Game Ready drivers are optimized for day-one game launches and have caused stability regressions in creative apps like Resolve.</p>
</div>






